          "text": "4.5 TORQUE OR MOMENT OF FORGE\n\nForce can be used to produce rotation in an object, for example in opening a door or tightening a nut with spanner or wrench. Turning effect produced in body about a fixed point due to applied force is called torque or moment of force. Physicists usually use the term \"torque,\" while engineers usually use \"moment\".\n\nFigure 4.12 DOOR PHYSICS\n\nTorque is the cause of changes in rotational motion and is similar to force, which causes changes in translational motion. This means that torque play the same role in rotation as force in translation.\n\nQuantitatively Torque = perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation x force applied. This perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to the line of action of force is called moment arm (or lever arm) 'd'.\n\nThus torque 'T' will be the product of moment arm 'd' and applied force 'F'.\n\nT= dx F\n\n4.7\n\nThe SI unit of torque is \"newton\n\n·A·\n\naxis of rotation\n\nd\n\nF\n\nFor example, to open the door a PERSPECTIVE VIEW\n\nmetre\" (Nm).\n\nforce 'F' is applied at perpendicular distance 'd' from the axis of rotation as shown in the figure 4.12. Increasing the\n\naxis of rotation\n\nd\n\nF\n\nb\n\nTOP VIEW\n\napplied force 'F' or the moment arm 'd' increases the torque 'T'. Reducing applied force 'F' or moment arm 'd' decreases torque 'T'.For example if we apply more force we will forcefully produce rotation in the door and small force at the same distance from the axis of rotation will produce less torque. Similarly as we move away from the hinges (where there is axis of rotation) moment arm 'd'\n\nF\n\nS\n\nTurning Effect of Forces\n\nincreases and torque also increases for same applied force. Furthermore, if there is no force no torque will be produced, similarly a force applied at the pivot point\n\nwill cause no torque since the moment arm would be zero (d = 0).\n\nHowever force is not always applied perpendicularly, in this situation we have to extend the line of action of force applied 'F' and take the moment arm 'd' as perpendicular distance from the axis of rotation to the line of action of force.\n\nFigure 4.13 MOMENT ARM\n\na\n\nb\n\nForce\n\nAxis of\n\nrotation\n\nAxis of rotation\n\nF\n\nNut\n\nForce F\n\nNut\n\n90°\n\nLine of action of force\n\n90°\n\nd. Moment arm\n\nLine of action of force\n\nd. Moment arm\n\nFor example consider a wrench which is used to open a nut as shown in figure 4.13 (a) the torque is produced. However if we continue to apply the same force in the same direction, after the nut has rotated as in figure 4.13 (b), our definition of moment arm will reduce it in magnitude and torque will decrease.\n\nMoment arm is key to the operation of the lever, pulley, gear, and many other simple machines.\n\nThere are two senses of rotation. If the force is capable of rotating the body in clockwise direction, the torque is known as clockwise torque.\n\nSimilarly, if the force is capable of producing rotation in the anti-clockwise direction, the torque is known as anti-\n\nclockwise torque.\n\nConventionally, clockwise torque is taken as negative, whereas\n\nanticlockwise torque is taken as positive.\n\nPage\n\n113\n\nTurning Effect of Forces\n\nOPENING A DOOR\n\nExample 4.3\n\nIn Figure a force (magnitude 55 N) is applied to a door. However, the arms are different in the three parts of the drawing: (a) d1 = 0.80 m, (b) d = 0.60 m, and (c)\n\nd=0m. Find the torque in each case.\n\na\n\nline of action of force\n\nb\n\naxis of\n\nline of action\n\nof force\n\nC\n\naxis of\n\nrotation\n\nd = 0.8 m\n\nA\n\n90°\n\nrotation\n\nF\n\nd1\n\n= 0.6 mi\n\naxis of rotation\n\nline of action\n\nof force\n\nF\n\nde=0m\n\nF\n\nC\n\nB\n\nS GIVEN:\n\n0 Force F= 55 N\n\nMoment arm d1 = 0.80 m\n\nU Moment arm d1 = 0.60 m\n\nMoment arm d= 0.00 m\n\nREQUIRED:\n\n(a) torque T1 = ?\n\n(b) torque t1 = ?\n\n(c) torque tc = ?\n\nIn each case the lever arm is the perpendicular distance between the axis of\n\nO rotation and the line of action of the force.\n\nN\n\n(a) Using the definition of torque, the equation 4.7 can be written as\n\nTA=FxdA\n\nputting values\n\ntherefore\n\nTA=55 Nx0.80m\n\nTA=44Nm\n\nAnswer\n\n(b) Using the definition of torque, the equation 4.7 can be written as\n\nTB=Fxdg\n\nputting values\n\ntherefore\n\nTB=55 Nx0.60m\n\nTB=33Nm\n\nAnswer\n\n(c) Using the definition of torque, the equation 4.7 can be written as\n\nputting values\n\ntherefore\n\nTc=Fxdc\n\nTc=55 Nx0.00m\n\nTc =0Nm\n\nAnswer\n\nBecause the lever arm is different in each case, the torque is different, even\n\nPage\n\n114\n\nTurning Effect of Forces\n\nthough the magnitude of the applied force is the same. In parts a and b the torques are positive, since the forces tend to produce a counter clockwise rotation of the door. In part c the line of action of force passes through the axis of rotation (the hinge). Hence, the lever arm is zero, and the torque is zero.. Assignment 4.3 FORCE ON BOTTLE OPENER\n\n20 Nm torque is required to open a soda bottle. A boy with a bottle opener apply a force perpendicularly at 0.1 m, what is the magnitude of force required."
