        "longQuestions": [
          {
            "question": "Explain the resolution of forces. Derive mathematical formulas for the rectangular components of a force, and explain how a force is determined from its components.",
            "answer": "1. Definition: Resolution of forces is the process of replacing a single force vector with two or more perpendicular component vectors that produce the exact same mechanical effect.\n\n2. Derivation of Rectangular Components:\nConsider a force $\\vec{F}$ represented by line segment $OA$ making an angle $\\theta$ with the positive x-axis. From point $A$, drop perpendicular $AB$ onto the x-axis, and perpendicular $AC$ onto the y-axis.\n- By head-to-tail rule: $\\vec{F} = \\vec{F}_x + \\vec{F}_y$.\n- In right-angled triangle $OBA$:\n  $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{\\text{Base}}{\\text{Hypotenuse}} = \\frac{OB}{OA} = \\frac{F_x}{F} \\implies F_x = F\\cos\\theta$.\n  $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{\\text{Perpendicular}}{\\text{Hypotenuse}} = \\frac{AB}{OA} = \\frac{F_y}{F} \\implies F_y = F\\sin\\theta$.\n\n3. Determination of Force from its Components:\nIf rectangular components $F_x$ and $F_y$ are known:\n- Magnitude: In triangle $OBA$, by Pythagorean theorem:\n  $OA^2 = OB^2 + AB^2 \\implies F^2 = F_x^2 + F_y^2 \\implies F = \\sqrt{F_x^2 + F_y^2}$.\n- Direction (Angle $\\theta$):\n  $\\tan\\theta = \\frac{\\text{Perpendicular}}{\\text{Base}} = \\frac{F_y}{F_x} \\implies \\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{F_y}{F_x}\\right)$.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 4 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "State and explain the two conditions of equilibrium in detail. Give two examples of bodies satisfying both conditions.",
            "answer": "1. First Condition of Equilibrium (Translational Equilibrium):\n'The vector sum of all external forces acting on a body must be zero.'\n$\\sum \\vec{F} = 0$.\nIn a two-dimensional Cartesian plane, this breaks into two scalar equations:\n$\\sum F_x = 0$ (Sum of forces along x-axis is zero)\n$\\sum F_y = 0$ (Sum of forces along y-axis is zero).\nIf only the first condition is satisfied, the body has no translational acceleration ($a = 0$), but it may still rotate (e.g., forces in a couple satisfy $\\sum F = 0$ but produce rotation).\n\n2. Second Condition of Equilibrium (Rotational Equilibrium):\n'The algebraic sum of all external torques acting on a body about any axis of rotation must be zero.'\n$\\sum \\vec{\\tau} = 0$.\nSum of clockwise torques must equal sum of anticlockwise torques:\n$\\sum \\tau_{\\text{clockwise}} = \\sum \\tau_{\\text{anticlockwise}}$.\nThis guarantees that the body possesses no angular acceleration ($\\alpha = 0$).\n\n3. Complete Equilibrium:\nA body is in complete equilibrium if and only if both the First Condition and Second Condition are simultaneously satisfied.\nExamples:\n- A book lying at rest on a horizontal study table ($W = R$ so $\\sum F_y = 0$, lines of action coincide so $\\sum \\tau = 0$).\n- A framed photograph suspended symmetrically from a wall nail by two strings.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 4 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Explain the experiment to determine the center of gravity of an irregular planar lamina using a plumb line with step-by-step procedure and labeled diagram.",
            "answer": "1. Apparatus: Irregularly shaped cardboard lamina, plumb line (a thin thread tied to a pointed metallic bob), nail or pin clamped in a stand, pencil, and ruler.\n\n2. Working Principle: When a body is freely suspended from a pivot point, it swings until its center of gravity falls vertically beneath the suspension point so that gravitational torque is zero.\n\n3. Experimental Procedure:\n   a. Pierce three small pinholes A, B, and C near the outer edges of the irregular cardboard lamina.\n   b. Suspend the lamina freely from hole A using a pin clamped in the laboratory stand so it swings without friction.\n   c. Hang the plumb line from the same pin support in front of the lamina.\n   d. When the bob settles, mark two dots along the thread on the cardboard and draw straight line $AD$ using a ruler.\n   e. Repeat the exact suspension procedure from hole B to draw straight line $BE$.\n   f. Repeat once more from hole C to draw line $CF$.\n\n4. Observation and Conclusion:\nAll three lines $AD$, $BE$, and $CF$ intersect at a single unique common point $G$. This intersection point $G$ is the Centre of Gravity of the irregular lamina.\n5. Verification: The cardboard can be perfectly balanced horizontally on the tip of a pencil placed directly under point $G$.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 4 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Discuss the three states of equilibrium (stable, unstable, and neutral) in terms of center of gravity behavior, potential energy, and restoring torques with diagrams.",
            "answer": "1. Stable Equilibrium:\n- Definition: A body is in stable equilibrium if it returns to its original position after a slight disturbance.\n- Center of Gravity: Initially at its lowest possible position. When tilted, the CG rises.\n- Potential Energy: Minimum in equilibrium; increases upon tilt.\n- Mechanics: The line of action of weight falls inside the base area, exerting a restoring torque that pulls the body back.\n- Example: A cone standing upright on its circular base, a box on a floor.\n\n2. Unstable Equilibrium:\n- Definition: A body is in unstable equilibrium if it does not return to its original position when slightly disturbed, but topples over.\n- Center of Gravity: Initially at its highest position. When tilted, the CG lowers.\n- Potential Energy: Maximum; decreases upon disturbance.\n- Mechanics: The line of action of weight immediately falls outside the tiny base point, generating an overturning torque.\n- Example: A cone balanced on its vertex tip, a pen balanced on its nib.\n\n3. Neutral Equilibrium:\n- Definition: A body is in neutral equilibrium if, when disturbed, it remains in equilibrium in its new position.\n- Center of Gravity: Stays at the exact same horizontal height throughout.\n- Potential Energy: Remains constant.\n- Mechanics: Weight and normal reaction remain collinear and balanced at every position.\n- Example: A ball rolling on a smooth horizontal floor, a cylinder lying on its side.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 4 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "What is torque? Describe clockwise and anticlockwise torques, moment arm, and solve the seesaw balance problem based on the principle of moments.",
            "answer": "1. Torque (Moment of a Force):\nTorque measures the capability of an applied force to rotate a body about a pivot: $\\tau = F \\times d$.\nWhere $F$ is the magnitude of the force and $d$ is the moment arm (the perpendicular distance from pivot to line of action of force).\n\n2. Direction Conventions:\n- Clockwise Torque: Tends to rotate the body clockwise (negative sign).\n- Anticlockwise Torque: Tends to rotate the body anticlockwise (positive sign).\n\n3. Principle of Moments:\nFor a body to be in rotational equilibrium: $\\sum \\tau_{\\text{clockwise}} = \\sum \\tau_{\\text{anticlockwise}}$.\n\n4. Seesaw Problem (Textbook Example):\nTwo children, Sanaan of weight $W_S$ and Romaisa of weight $W_R = 600\\text{ N}$, sit on a seesaw pivoted at the center.\nRomaisa sits at distance $d_R = 1.0\\text{ m}$ from the pivot.\nSanaan sits on the opposite side at distance $d_S = 1.5\\text{ m}$ from the pivot.\nFor balance:\n$\\text{Anticlockwise Torque} = \\text{Clockwise Torque}$\n$W_S \\times d_S = W_R \\times d_R$\n$W_S \\times 1.5 = 600 \\times 1.0 = 600\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}$\n$W_S = \\frac{600}{1.5} = 400\\text{ N}$.\nThus, Sanaan's weight must be $400\\text{ N}$ (mass $\\approx 40\\text{ kg}$).",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 4 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          }
        ]
