          "text": "5.2 MASS OF EARTH\n\nThe determination of gravitational constant helped scientists to calculate the mass of earth, sun and other celestial objects.\n\nLet an object of mass 'mo' be placed on the surface of the Earth. The distance between the center of the body and the earth is (nearly) equal to the radius of earth 'r'. If the mass of earth is 'me' then the force F, with which the earth attracts the body towards its centre is given by law of gravitation.\n\nF2 = G\n\nmome\n\nΓΕ\n\n2\n\nFigure 5.4 MASS OF EARTH\n\nMagnified object\n\nWe know that the force of gravity is equal to the weight of the body.\n\nF2 =W = mog\n\ncomparing equation 1 and equation\n\n2 we get\n\nm。 g = G\n\nor\n\n8=GME\n\nΓε\n\n2\n\nmo ME\n\n2\n\nre-arranging ME\n\nГЕ\n\ngre2\n\nG\n\n5.3\n\nradius of\n\nTAS Earth 'r'\n\n1818\n\nObject of mass 'mo'\n\nCenter of\n\nEarth of mass 'm'\n\nEarth\n\nSince Gravitational Constant G= 6.673 x 10\" Nm'kg for earth we have acceleration due to gravity g = 9.8 ms (9.8 N kg') and radius of Earth r = 6.4 x 10° m. Putting these values in equation 5.3 we get\n\nGravitation\n\n9.8Nkg 1(6.4x10 m)2\n\nME\n\n6.67 x 10-11 Nm2 kg-2\n\nor\n\nME =\n\n9.8g1×4.1×10132\n\n6.67×10-11 m2 kg\n\nTIP\n\nNkg = ms\n\nSince N = kg ms2\n\nDividing both sides by kg\n\nN/kg = ms2\n\ntherefore ME=6×1024 kg\n\nor\n\nN kg'' = ms2\n\nThus the mass of earth is approximately 6 x 1024 kg. This means that if we know acceleration due to gravity 'g' and radius 'r' of any planet or star we can\n\ncalculate its mass.\n\nExample 5.2\n\nMASS OF JUPITER\n\nJupiter is the largest planet in the solar system. Radius of Jupiter is r,=7.15.x 10' m, the acceleration due to gravity on Jupiter is 24.7 N/kg. Calculate the mass of Jupiter.\n\nS\n\nGIVEN:\n\nO\n\nRadius of Jupiter r, = 7.15 x 10' m\n\nAcceleration due to gravity at Jupiter g, = 24.7N/kg Gravitational Constant G = 6.673 x 10\" Nm2kg2\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nMass of Jupiter m, =?\n\nThe mass of a planet or star can be calculated by using equation 4.4\n\n8,r,2\n\nmj\n\nO\n\nG\n\nN\n\nputting values\n\nmj\n\n24.7 N/kg (7.15 × 107 m)2\n\n6.673 x 10-11 Nm2kg 20\n\nor\n\nm1 =\n\n24.7 kg1×5.11x1015 m2\n\n6.673 x 10-11 Nm kg 2\n\n-2\n\nor\n\nm, = 1.891x1016+11kg\n\nmj = 1.89×1027 kg\n\nAnswer\n\ntherefore\n\nThus the mass of Jupiter is about 300 times the mass of the earth.\n\nAssignment 5.2 MASS OF MOON\n\nIf the radius of the moon is 1.74 x 10' m and have acceleration due to gravity on\n\nits surface as 1.6 ms2. Calculate the mass of moon.\n\nPage\n\n134\n\nGravitation\n\nTIP\n\nREMEMBER THESE VALUES\n\nGravitational constant\n\nG = 6.67 x 10\" N m2 kg\n\nr1 = 6.4 x 10° m\n\nRadius of earth\n\nTABLE 5.1: SELECTED DATA FOR THE SOLAR SYSTEM\n\nPlanet\n\nRadius (km) Mass (102 kg) g (m/s2)\n\nOrbital Period (yr)\n\nMercury\n\n2,400\n\n0.330\n\n3.7\n\n0.241\n\nVenus\n\n6,050\n\n4.87\n\n8.9\n\n0.615\n\nEarth\n\n6,370\n\n5.97\n\n9.8\n\n1\n\nMars\n\n3,400\n\n0.642\n\n3.7\n\n1.88\n\nJupiter\n\n71,500\n\n1890\n\n24.7\n\n11.9\n\nSaturn\n\n60,300\n\n568\n\n9.0\n\n29.4\n\nUranus\n\n25,600\n\n86.8\n\n8.7\n\n83.8\n\nNeptune\n\n24,800\n\n102\n\n11.0\n\n164\n\nSun\n\n696,000\n\n1,990,000\n\n274\n\nINFORMATION"
