          "text": "5.4 VARIATION of 'g' WITH ALTITUDE\n\nThe value of 'g' at a given place depends Figure 5.6\n\nupon the distance from the centre of earth. Greater the distance from the centre of the earth,\n\nsmaller will be the value of 'g'. Equation 5.4 shows that the value of 'g' varies inversely as the square of distance. Let 'g' be the value of acceleration due to garvity at a height 'h' from the surface of the earth we can modify Equation 5.4 as.\n\nSh=G\n\nME\n\n1\n\n(rE+ h)2\n\nequation 5.3 can also be written as\n\ng=G\n\nME\n\nor\n\nGME = grz\n\n2\n\nh\n\nTE\n\nEarth\n\nputting equation 2 in equation 1, we get Sh=\n\ngrz (rE+ h)\n\n5.5\n\nEquation 5.5 shows that as we move away from the center of earth the value of 'g' decreases. The change in the value of g is significant only at very large\n\ndistances.\n\nPage\n\n139\n\nExample 5.4\n\nGravitation\n\nMETEOROID IN EARTH'S FIELD\n\nAmeteoroid (a chunk of rock) is at 4.4 x 10' m from the earth, what is the value of free fall acceleration 'g' at this point due to earth?\n\nS\n\nGIVEN:\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nO\n\nRadius of earth r1 = 6.4 x 10° m\n\nAcceleration due to gravity g = 9.8 ms\n\nfree-fall acceleration at\n\nheight g1 =?\n\nU\n\nheight h = 4.4 x 10' m = 44 x 10° m\n\nON\n\nThe value of 'g' at height h, can be calculated by using equation 5.5\n\nSh\n\n=\n\nputting values Sh\n\n=\n\n3r2\n\n(TE+ h)\n\n9.8 ms2 x (6.4 x 106 m)2 (6.4 × 10° m +44 x 106 m)2\n\n9.8 ms2 x (6.4 x 106 m)2 (50.4 × 106 m)2\n\nEXTENSION EXERCISE 5.1\n\nor\n\nSh\n\nor\n\nᏚᏂ\n\n9.8 ms2 x 4.1x1013 m2 2.54 × 1015 m2\n\ntherefore\n\nSh=0.16ms-2\n\nAnswer\n\nConfirm the value of 'g' for Jupiter given in Example 5.2, Using equation 5.5\n\nAssignment 5.4\n\nValue Of g At Specific Height\n\nCalculate the value of g at 1000 km arid 35900 km above the earth surface.\n\nTABLE 5.1: CHANGE IN GRAVITATIONAL\n\nACCELERATION WITH ALTITUDE\n\nAltitude (km) a (m/s) Altitude Example\n\nPOINT TO PONDER\n\n0.0\n\n9.8\n\n8.8\n\n9.8\n\nMean Earth adius\n\nMt. Everest\n\n36.6\n\n9.7\n\n400\n\n8.7\n\n35 700\n\n0.2\n\nHighest manned balloon Space shuttle orbit\n\nCommunications satellite\n\nThe magnitude of g on the moon's surface is about 1/6 of the value of g on Earth's surface. Can you infer from this relationship that the moon's mass is 1/6 of Earth's mass? Why or why not?\n\nPage\n\n140\n\nGravitation"
