        "5.1 Law of Universal Gravitation\n\nGravitation\n\nThe study of gravity has always been a central theme in physics, from Galileo's early experiments on free fall in the seventeenth century to Stephen Hawking's work on black holes in recent years. Perhaps the grandest milestone in this endeavor, was the discovery by Newton the law for gravitation.\n\nOur Milky Way galaxy is a disk-shaped collection of gas, dust, and billions of stars, including our Sun and solar system. Image shows how our galaxy would look if we could view it from outside. Earth is near the edge of the disk of the galaxy, about 26,000 light-years (2.5 x 1020 m) from its central bulge. Our galaxy is a member of the Local Group of galaxies, which includes the Andromeda galaxy at a distance of 2.5 x 10° light-years.\n\nThe force that binds together these progressively larger structures, from moon all the way to galaxy clusters is known as the gravitational force. Gravity is action-at-a-distance force which acts even when the interacting objects are not in physical contact. This force not only holds us on Earth but also reaches out across intergalactic space and acts between galaxies. This force is our focus in this unit.\n\n5.1 LAW OF UNIVERSAL GRAVITATION\n\nNewton expressed the force of attraction between different bodies in the universe in the form of a law known as the law of universal gravitation; which is stated as follows\n\n'Every body in the universe attracts every other body with a force which is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between their centres'.\n\nConsider two spherical bodies of masses 'm,' and 'm,' separated by distance 'r' as shown in figure 5.1.\n\nBy definition of Newton's law of universal gravitation, the force of\n\ngravity F, is\n\nFg ∞ m1 x m2\n\nFigure 5.1\n\nUNIVERSAL GRAVITATION\n\n1\n\n1\n\nand\n\nFs\n\n2\n\nCentre\n\n2\n\ncombining equation 1 and\n\nequation 2 we get\n\nF. ∞\n\nm1 m2\n\nm,\n\n14\n\nF\n\nm2\n\nGravitation\n\nTo change sign of proportionality into equality a constant is included\n\n=\n\nr2\n\n5.1\n\nFigure 5.2) TORSION BALANCE\n\nWhere G is constant of proportionality and is known as gravitational constant. The value of gravitational constant (as determine by experiment) G = 6.67 × 10\" Nm2 kg2. It does not depend on the medium (vacuum, air, water, concrete etc.) between the two bodies. The above formula is a mathematical expression of Newton's law of universal gravitation.\n\nNewton's Third Law of Motion and Universal Gravitation:\n\nWe can see that the force acting on mass m, due to mass m, is Fr. Also the force acting on mass m, due to mass m, is same force F2, both these forces are equal but opposite in direction. Therefore, we can say that the forces acting on two bodies due to gravitational force is the example of action and reaction forces.\n\nF12 = −F12\n\n5.2\n\n3\n\nFiber\n\nMirror\n\nM m\n\nLight source\n\nPiyot\n\nM\n\nwwwwwwwww\n\nScale\n\nCavendish used an apparatus like this to measure the gravitational attraction between the two suspended spheres ( m ) and the two on the stand (M) by observing the amount of torsion (twisting) created in the fiber. Distance between the masses can be varied to check the dependence of the force on distance. Modern experiments of this type continue to explore gravity.\n\nFigure 5.3 UNIVERSAL GRAVITATION AND NEWTON'S THIRD LAW OF MOTION\n\nFor example, Earth pulls on the Moon and the Moon pulls Centre on Earth with a force of equal magnitude. At Earth's surface, Earth pulls down on a 1.0 kg mass with a force of magnitude 9.8 N, and the\n\nr\n\n142\n\nF21\n\nm2\n\nm,\n\nPage\n\n131\n\nGravitation\n\n1.0 kg mass pulls upward on Earth with a Graph 5.1 force of magnitude 9.8 N.\n\nForce (N)\n\nF\n\nThe gravitational force is an inverse- square force: it decreases by a factor of 4 when the distance increases by a factor of 2, it decreases by a factor of 9 when the distance increases by a factor of 3, and so on. Graph 5.1 is a plot of the magnitude of 1/4 F the gravitational force as a function of the F\n\ndistance.\n\n%,\n\nDistance\n\n(m)\n\n++\n\n+\n\n0\n\nr 2r\n\n3r\n\nExample 5.1 CAN YOU ATTRACT ANOTHER PERSON GRAVITATIONALLY Suppose you have mass of 40 kg and a 45 kg person is sitting on a bench close to you, such that the distance between centers of you and the person is 0.5 m. Estimate the magnitude of the gravitational force you exert on that person. S GIVEN:\n\n=\n\nmass m, 40 kg\n\n0\n\nmass m2 = 45 kg\n\nL\n\ndistance r = 0.5 m\n\nU Gravitational Constant G = 6.673 x 10\" Nm'kg2\n\nT\n\nBy Newton's law of universal gravitation\n\nREQUIRED: Gravitational force F1 =?\n\nJos sona eris 02A\n\nO\n\nF = G\n\nN\n\nm1 m2 r2\n\nputting values\n\nor\n\ntherefore\n\nFg = 6.67 × 1011 Nm2kg·2 40 kg× 45 kg\n\n(0.5m)236.\n\nFg = 6.67 x 10-11 Nr2 1800 kg us to ok\n\nFg = 4.8×10-7 N\n\nkg 0.25m\n\nAnswer\n\nThis means we attract each other but a force of about 10 N is unnoticeably small unless extremely sensitive instruments are used. Even very large scale\n\nobjects like ships and building have very small gravitational attraction.\n\nAssignment 5.1 GRAVITATIONAL FORCE ON MOON\n\nThe mass of earth is 6 x 102 kg and that of the moon is 7.4 x 1022 kg. If the distance between the earth and the moon is 3.84 x 10' km, calculate the force exerted by\n\nthe earth on the moon.\n\nPage\n\n132\n\nINFORMATION\n\nGravitation\n\nLight from distant stars and galaxies takes so long to reach us that we are actually seeing these objects as they appeared in the past. As we look up at the sky, we are really looking back in time. For example, the Sun's light takes almost 8.5 minutes to travel to Earth, so we see the Sun as it looked 8.5 minutes ago. The nearest star to us, Proxima Centauri, is 4.2 light- years away, so it appears as it was 4.2 years ago. The nearest galaxy is 2.5 million light-years away, and it looks as it did when our australopithecus hominid ancestors walked the planet. The farther away something is, the further back in time it appears.",
        "5.2 Mass of Earth\n\n5.2 MASS OF EARTH\n\nThe determination of gravitational constant helped scientists to calculate the mass of earth, sun and other celestial objects.\n\nLet an object of mass 'mo' be placed on the surface of the Earth. The distance between the center of the body and the earth is (nearly) equal to the radius of earth 'r'. If the mass of earth is 'me' then the force F, with which the earth attracts the body towards its centre is given by law of gravitation.\n\nF2 = G\n\nmome\n\nΓΕ\n\n2\n\nFigure 5.4 MASS OF EARTH\n\nMagnified object\n\nWe know that the force of gravity is equal to the weight of the body.\n\nF2 =W = mog\n\ncomparing equation 1 and equation\n\n2 we get\n\nm。 g = G\n\nor\n\n8=GME\n\nΓε\n\n2\n\nmo ME\n\n2\n\nre-arranging ME\n\nГЕ\n\ngre2\n\nG\n\n5.3\n\nradius of\n\nTAS Earth 'r'\n\n1818\n\nObject of mass 'mo'\n\nCenter of\n\nEarth of mass 'm'\n\nEarth\n\nSince Gravitational Constant G= 6.673 x 10\" Nm'kg for earth we have acceleration due to gravity g = 9.8 ms (9.8 N kg') and radius of Earth r = 6.4 x 10° m. Putting these values in equation 5.3 we get\n\nGravitation\n\n9.8Nkg 1(6.4x10 m)2\n\nME\n\n6.67 x 10-11 Nm2 kg-2\n\nor\n\nME =\n\n9.8g1×4.1×10132\n\n6.67×10-11 m2 kg\n\nTIP\n\nNkg = ms\n\nSince N = kg ms2\n\nDividing both sides by kg\n\nN/kg = ms2\n\ntherefore ME=6×1024 kg\n\nor\n\nN kg'' = ms2\n\nThus the mass of earth is approximately 6 x 1024 kg. This means that if we know acceleration due to gravity 'g' and radius 'r' of any planet or star we can\n\ncalculate its mass.\n\nExample 5.2\n\nMASS OF JUPITER\n\nJupiter is the largest planet in the solar system. Radius of Jupiter is r,=7.15.x 10' m, the acceleration due to gravity on Jupiter is 24.7 N/kg. Calculate the mass of Jupiter.\n\nS\n\nGIVEN:\n\nO\n\nRadius of Jupiter r, = 7.15 x 10' m\n\nAcceleration due to gravity at Jupiter g, = 24.7N/kg Gravitational Constant G = 6.673 x 10\" Nm2kg2\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nMass of Jupiter m, =?\n\nThe mass of a planet or star can be calculated by using equation 4.4\n\n8,r,2\n\nmj\n\nO\n\nG\n\nN\n\nputting values\n\nmj\n\n24.7 N/kg (7.15 × 107 m)2\n\n6.673 x 10-11 Nm2kg 20\n\nor\n\nm1 =\n\n24.7 kg1×5.11x1015 m2\n\n6.673 x 10-11 Nm kg 2\n\n-2\n\nor\n\nm, = 1.891x1016+11kg\n\nmj = 1.89×1027 kg\n\nAnswer\n\ntherefore\n\nThus the mass of Jupiter is about 300 times the mass of the earth.\n\nAssignment 5.2 MASS OF MOON\n\nIf the radius of the moon is 1.74 x 10' m and have acceleration due to gravity on\n\nits surface as 1.6 ms2. Calculate the mass of moon.\n\nPage\n\n134\n\nGravitation\n\nTIP\n\nREMEMBER THESE VALUES\n\nGravitational constant\n\nG = 6.67 x 10\" N m2 kg\n\nr1 = 6.4 x 10° m\n\nRadius of earth\n\nTABLE 5.1: SELECTED DATA FOR THE SOLAR SYSTEM\n\nPlanet\n\nRadius (km) Mass (102 kg) g (m/s2)\n\nOrbital Period (yr)\n\nMercury\n\n2,400\n\n0.330\n\n3.7\n\n0.241\n\nVenus\n\n6,050\n\n4.87\n\n8.9\n\n0.615\n\nEarth\n\n6,370\n\n5.97\n\n9.8\n\n1\n\nMars\n\n3,400\n\n0.642\n\n3.7\n\n1.88\n\nJupiter\n\n71,500\n\n1890\n\n24.7\n\n11.9\n\nSaturn\n\n60,300\n\n568\n\n9.0\n\n29.4\n\nUranus\n\n25,600\n\n86.8\n\n8.7\n\n83.8\n\nNeptune\n\n24,800\n\n102\n\n11.0\n\n164\n\nSun\n\n696,000\n\n1,990,000\n\n274\n\nINFORMATION",
        "5.3 Gravitational Field\n\n5.3 GRAVITATIONAL FIELD\n\nGravity is a field force:\n\nNewton was unable to explain how objects can exert forces on one another without coming into contact. He developed a mathematical theory to describe gravity, but he did not had a physical explanation for how gravity works. Scientists later developed a theory of fields to explain how gravity and other forces\n\noperate.\n\nAccording to this theory, masses create a gravitational field in space. (Similarly, charged\n\nWe have eight planets in our Solar\n\nSystem. However, outside of our Solar System there are thousands\n\nof other planets. The extra-solar\n\nplanets or exo-planets are in\n\norbit around another star. So far\n\nobjects generate an electric field and magnets we have almost 3500 confirmed develop\n\nawaiting confirmation.\n\nPage\n\n135\n\nan interaction between a mass and the Figure 5.5 Gravitational Field\n\ngravitational field created by other masses.\n\nAt any point, Earth's gravitational field can be described by the gravitational field strength, abbreviated as g. The value of 'g' is equal to the magnitude of the gravitational force exerted on a unit mass at that point,\n\nor\n\ng=F/m.\n\nThe gravitational field (g) is a vector with a magnitude of 'g' that points in the direction of the gravitational force.\n\nGravitational field strength (free-fall acceleration) 'g':\n\nConsider an object that is free to accelerate and is acted on only by gravitational force. According to Newton's second law, a = F/m. As seen earlier, g is defined as F,/m, where F, is gravitational force. Thus, the value of 'g' at any given point is equal to the acceleration due to gravity. For this reason, g = 9.8 m/s2 on Earth's surface. Although gravitational field\n\ng\n\ng\n\nWeaker F,\n\nStronger F\n\nstrength and free-fall acceleration are equivalent, they are not the same thing. For instance, when you hang an object from a spring scale, you are measuring gravitational field strength. Because the mass is at rest (in a frame of reference fixed to Earth's surface), there is no measurable acceleration.\n\nFigure 5.5 shows gravitational field vectors at different points around Earth. As shown in the figure, gravitational field strength rapidly decreases as the distance from Earth increases, as you would expect from the inverse-square nature of Newton's law of universal gravitation.\n\nWeight changes with location:\n\nIn Unit 3 Dynamics, we learned that weight is the magnitude of the force due to gravity, which equals mass times free-fall acceleration. We can now refine our definition of weight as mass times gravitational field strength.\n\nPage\n\n136\n\nGravitation\n\nThe two definitions are mathematically equivalent, but our new definition helps to explain why your weight changes with our location in the universe.\n\nNewton's law of universal gravitation shows that the value of g depends on mass of all reacting bodies and distance to it.\n\nFor example, consider an object (e.g. tennis ball) of mass m, placed on surface of earth. Let me be the mass of the earth and r, is the distance between their centers. The gravitational force between the tennis ball and Earth is as\n\nfollows:\n\nF1 =\n\nG\n\nmo me\n\n1\n\nThe gravitational field strength Fg by Newton's\n\nsecond law is\n\nF2 =W = mog\n\n2\n\ncomparing equation 1 and equation 2 we get\n\nmg=G\n\n= GMME\n\n2\n\nTID BITS\n\ntherefore\n\nГЕ\n\ng=G\n\nME\n\n2\n\nInserting the known values of\n\n5.4\n\nm2 = 6×1024 kg\n\nand\n\nr2 = 6.4 × 10° m\n\nalong with\n\nG = 6.67 × 1011 Nm2kg2,\n\nwe find\n\ng= 6.67 × 10-11 Nm2kg 2\n\n6×1024 kg (6.4 x 106 m)2\n\ntherefore art\n\ng=9.77 ms\n\n-2\n\nor\n\ng = 9.8ms-2\n\nThe hottest planet is not the closest planet to the Sun. Even though Mercury is the closest planet to the Sun, it is not actually the hottest. Mercury does not have any atmosphere meaning that this planet is only hot in the daytime when it is directly facing the Sun. At this stage temperatures can rise to 425°C but at night the planet's temperature can drop down to a freezing 180°C. Venus (shown in picture)\n\nis the hottest planet. Its thick clouds trap the Sun's heat causing Venus to be a sizzling 500 Call of the time!\n\nEquation 5.4 shows that the value of 'g' does not depend upon the mass of the earth with the same acceleration. This equation also shows that gravitational the body. This means that light and heavy bodies should fall towards the centre of field strength depends only on mass of earth 'm,' and radius of earth 'r'. As earth\n\nPage\n\n137\n\nU\n\nGravitation\n\nis not a perfect sphere, and there are mountains and trenches so our distance from Earth's center varies as we change location, so our weight also varies. On the surface of any planet, the value of g, as well as our weight, will depend on the\n\nplanet's mass and radius.\n\nTECHNOLOGY\n\nThe variation in the value of 'g' on Earth is used to detect the presence of minerals and oil. Geophysicists uses sensitive instrument, called gravimeters, to detect small variations in 'g' when they search for new deposits of ore or oil. Gold and silver deposits increase the value of 'g', while deposits of oil and natural gas decrease 'g'. Picture is an example of a map that shows different measured\n\nvalues of gas lines, where each line represents a specific value of g.\n\nExample 5.3\n\nVALUE OF 'g' AT SUN'S SURFACE\n\nWhat is the free-fall acceleration at the surface of the sun? As mass of Sun is 1.99 x 1030 kg and having radius of 6.96 x 10m.\n\nS GIVEN:\n\nRadius of Sun r1 = 6.96 × 10° m\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nfree-fall acceleration at Sun g1 =?\n\nGravitational Constant G = 6.67 x 10\" Nm2 kg2\n\nX\n\nMass of Sun m1 = 1.99 × 1030 kg\n\nT The value of g at sun's surface can be calculated by equation 5.4 as\n\ng=G\n\nG 2\n\nms\n\nO\n\nN\n\ng=6.67x1011 Nm2kg 2\n\n1.99 × 1030 kg\n\n(6.96 × 108 m)2\n\ntherefore 8 = 274ms-2\n\nAnswer\n\nNotice that the mass of the Sun is almost a million times more than the mass of the Earth, and having gravitational acceleration of about 30 times that of\n\nearth.\n\nAssignment 5.3 FREE FALL AT MOON\n\nAn astronaut of mass 65.0 kg (weighing 637 N on earth) is walking on the surface of the Moon, which has a mean radius of 1.74 x 10° m and a mass of 7.35 × 1022 kg. What is the weight of the astronaut on moon? What is the free-fall acceleration\n\nat the surface of the moon?\n\nPage\n\n138\n\nGravitation\n\nINFORMATION CHART\n\nThis global model of the Earth's gravitational strength was constructed from a combination of surface gravity measurements and satellite tracking data. It shows how the acceleration of gravity varies from the value at an idealized \"sea level\" that takes into account the Earth's non-spherical shape. (The Earth is somewhat flattened at the poles-its radius is greatest at the equator.) Gravity is strongest in the red areas and weakest in the dark blue areas.",
        "5.4 Variation of 'g' with Altitude\n\n5.4 VARIATION of 'g' WITH ALTITUDE\n\nThe value of 'g' at a given place depends Figure 5.6\n\nupon the distance from the centre of earth. Greater the distance from the centre of the earth,\n\nsmaller will be the value of 'g'. Equation 5.4 shows that the value of 'g' varies inversely as the square of distance. Let 'g' be the value of acceleration due to garvity at a height 'h' from the surface of the earth we can modify Equation 5.4 as.\n\nSh=G\n\nME\n\n1\n\n(rE+ h)2\n\nequation 5.3 can also be written as\n\ng=G\n\nME\n\nor\n\nGME = grz\n\n2\n\nh\n\nTE\n\nEarth\n\nputting equation 2 in equation 1, we get Sh=\n\ngrz (rE+ h)\n\n5.5\n\nEquation 5.5 shows that as we move away from the center of earth the value of 'g' decreases. The change in the value of g is significant only at very large\n\ndistances.\n\nPage\n\n139\n\nExample 5.4\n\nGravitation\n\nMETEOROID IN EARTH'S FIELD\n\nAmeteoroid (a chunk of rock) is at 4.4 x 10' m from the earth, what is the value of free fall acceleration 'g' at this point due to earth?\n\nS\n\nGIVEN:\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nO\n\nRadius of earth r1 = 6.4 x 10° m\n\nAcceleration due to gravity g = 9.8 ms\n\nfree-fall acceleration at\n\nheight g1 =?\n\nU\n\nheight h = 4.4 x 10' m = 44 x 10° m\n\nON\n\nThe value of 'g' at height h, can be calculated by using equation 5.5\n\nSh\n\n=\n\nputting values Sh\n\n=\n\n3r2\n\n(TE+ h)\n\n9.8 ms2 x (6.4 x 106 m)2 (6.4 × 10° m +44 x 106 m)2\n\n9.8 ms2 x (6.4 x 106 m)2 (50.4 × 106 m)2\n\nEXTENSION EXERCISE 5.1\n\nor\n\nSh\n\nor\n\nᏚᏂ\n\n9.8 ms2 x 4.1x1013 m2 2.54 × 1015 m2\n\ntherefore\n\nSh=0.16ms-2\n\nAnswer\n\nConfirm the value of 'g' for Jupiter given in Example 5.2, Using equation 5.5\n\nAssignment 5.4\n\nValue Of g At Specific Height\n\nCalculate the value of g at 1000 km arid 35900 km above the earth surface.\n\nTABLE 5.1: CHANGE IN GRAVITATIONAL\n\nACCELERATION WITH ALTITUDE\n\nAltitude (km) a (m/s) Altitude Example\n\nPOINT TO PONDER\n\n0.0\n\n9.8\n\n8.8\n\n9.8\n\nMean Earth adius\n\nMt. Everest\n\n36.6\n\n9.7\n\n400\n\n8.7\n\n35 700\n\n0.2\n\nHighest manned balloon Space shuttle orbit\n\nCommunications satellite\n\nThe magnitude of g on the moon's surface is about 1/6 of the value of g on Earth's surface. Can you infer from this relationship that the moon's mass is 1/6 of Earth's mass? Why or why not?\n\nPage\n\n140\n\nGravitation",
        "5.5 Motion of Artificial Satellites\n\n5.5 MOTION OF ARTIFICIAL SATELLITES\n\nA Satellite is a naturally occurring or man-made object that orbits another object larger than itself by force of gravity.\n\nA natural body orbiting another body so large that center of mass is well within the larger body is called natural satellites of that body. Six of the major planets possess natural satellites often termed as moons.\n\nAny object purposely placed into orbit of earth or other planets, stars or sun are termed as artificial satellites. First artificial satellites was launched in 1957, since then thousands have been sent for communication industry (internet, mobile phones, TV communication etc.), weather, military purposes and scientific study.\n\nA satellite is put into orbit by moving it to high altitude and then accelerating it to a sufficiently high tangential speed with the use of rockets, as shown in Fig. 5.7. If the speed is too high, the spacecraft either move in elliptical orbit or will not be confined by the Earth's gravity and will escape, never to return. If the speed is too low, it will fall back to Earth. Satellites are typically put into\n\ncircular (or nearly circular) Figure 5.7 SATELLITE MOTION\n\norbits, because such orbits\n\nrequire the least take off speed.\n\nYou may be wondering \"What keeps a satellite up?\" The answer is: its high speed. If a satellite in orbit stop moving, it would fall directly to Earth. But at sufficient speed a satellite would orbit in space around a larger body.\n\nSpeed in circular orbit calculation:\n\nIn circular orbit a satellite\n\nhas a constant tangential speed called orbital velocity. Consider a satellite of mass m, revolving in a\n\nLow Speed\n\nSpeed adjusted\n\nso that satellite travel in circular path\n\nHigh speed\n\nVery high speed\n\n9totsvers\n\nW\n\ncircular orbit with velocity v from earth of mass me. Let r be the distance between\n\nPage\n\n141\n\nGravitation\n\nthe center of earth and the center of satellite as shown in figure 5.8. Then\n\ngravitational force by Newton's law of universal gravitation is\n\nFs\n\n=\n\nGmEms r2\n\nThen centripetal force to keep satellite of mass m, revolving in a circular orbi\n\nradius r with velocity 'v' is\n\nFc\n\nmsv2\n\n=\n\n(2\n\nr\n\nThen centripetal force is provided by gravitational force therefore\n\nF1 = Fc\n\n3\n\nPutting values from equation 1 and equation 2 in equation 3, we get\n\nor\n\n1/2v2 - Gryme T\n\n=\n\nbelard 2 GME 01 ods\n\n=\n\nr\n\ntaking square root on both sides\n\nFigure 5.8 ORBITAL SPEED\n\nms\n\nV\n\nr\n\nGME\n\n=\n\nGME\n\ntherefore\n\nV =\n\nor\n\nV =\n\nGME Vre+h\n\n5.6\n\nMc\n\nWhere h is the height of satellite from surface of earth and r, is the radiu\n\nearth. The equation 5.6 shows that orbital speed depends upon the mass of Ei and the distance from the center of the Earth to the center of mass of the satel\n\nand does not depend upon the mass of satellite.\n\nWhich means that for particular distance from the center of earth, all satellite should have the same orbital speed irrespective of the size of satellite\n\nPage\n\n142\n\nGravitation\n\nGeostationary communications satellites are placed in a circular orbit that is 3.59 x 10' m = 35.9 x 10° m above the surface of the earth. Their orbital speed can\n\nbe calculated by equation 5.6 as\n\nputting values\n\ntherefore\n\nV:\n\nGME VE+h\n\nv=6.673x1011 Nm2kg 2\n\n6x1024 kg (35.9+6.4)×106 m\n\nv = 3.07×103 ms-1\n\nor\n\nv = 3.07 kms-1\n\nThis equation can also be used to measure the velocity of any planet or celestial object moving in a circular path.\n\nExample 5.5\n\nSPEED OF EARTH AROUND SUN\n\nThe mass of the Sun is 1.99 × 1030 kg, and the radius of the Earth's orbit around the Sun is 1.5x10\" m. From this, calculate the orbital speed of the Earth.\n\nS GIVEN:\n\n0 Radius r=1.5x10\" m\n\nMass of Sun m,= 1.99 × 1030 kg\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nOrbital Speed v = ?\n\nN\n\nGravitational Constant G = 6.673 x 10\" Nm'kg20\n\nthe orbital speed is\n\nV=GMS\n\nputting values v =\n\nv=6.673 x 10-11 Nm2kg 2.\n\n√6.673x\n\n1.99×1030 kg\n\n1.5x1011 m\n\nAnswer\n\ntherefore\n\nv = 3.0x104 m/s = 30 km/s\n\nAssignment 5.5 ORBITAL SPEED OF A SATELLITE\n\nIf a satellite orbits the earth at 2,000 km above sea level, how fast must the orbiting satellite travel to maintain a circular orbit?\n\nT\n\nPRO\n\nGravitation\n\nK Law of Gravitation: Every object in the universe attracts every other E object with a force which is directly proportional to the product of their masses and inversely proportional to the square of the distance between\n\ntheir centres.\n\nP Gravitational acceleration: The acceleration of bodies falling freely towards the earth is called gravitational acceleration 'g'. The value of 'g'\n\ndecreases as altitude increases.\n\nN Artificial Satellite: The objects which are used for communication and space research revolve round the Earth under the force of gravity, which S provides the necessary centripetal force.\n\nOrbital Velocity: It is the velocity of a satellite which moves around the Earth at a specific height.\n\nGROUP-A\n\nSUPARCO: What is SUPARCO? Research Pakistan Space Program 2040 and Develop a presentation on space prospects in Pakistan?\n\nGROUP-B\n\nDream Space Visit: Research how do space scientists and engineers know what kinds of science instruments (cameras, spectrometers, etc.) to put on space craft that are destined for other planets, moons, asteroids, or comets? How do they decide what they will want to measure once they get to, say, Saturn's moon Titan or Jupiter's moon Europa? Select a place for your visit, then design a two day trip to that world, and describe the things you will carry with you. Write an essay and place it school library for reference.\n\nGROUP-C\n\nPluto: In 2006 a new definition of planet was set and Pluto was demoted to dwarf planet, research the reason and present it in chart.\n\nGROUP-D\n\nExtra Solar Planet: Explore the discovery of planets around other stars. What methods did the astronomers use? What measurements did they take?\n\nWrite a research article.\n\nGROUP-E\n\nSky Observation: Consult your instructor or other sources to find out what\n\nGravitation\n\nplanets are observable in the evening during the current month. Venus, Jupiter, or Mars are usually the best candidates.\n\na. Locate the planet visually and observe it with binoculars if possible. How\n\ndoes the planet differ in appearance from that of nearby stars?\n\nb. Sketch the position of the planet relative to nearby stars for several"
