        "rawExercise": "EXERCISE\nChoose the best possible answer:\nWork done will be zero when the angle between force and displacement is\nA. 30°\nB. 45°\nC. 60°\nD. 90°\n30 N force is exerted and the trolley moves a distance of 5 m in the direction of the force, the work done is\nA. 6J\nB. 25 J\nC. 150 J\nD. 0.17 N\nIf the speed of a car decreases by half, The kinetic energy change by\nfactor\nCHOICE\nA. 4\nB. 2\nC. 1/2\nD. 1/4\n4 An object of mass 10 kg is lifted vertically through a height of 5 m. The\ngravitational potential energy gained by the object is\nA. 0.5 J\nB. 2 J\nC. 50 J\nD. 490 J\nIf a petrol engine does 20 J of useful work for every 100 J of energy supplied to it, then its efficiency is\nU\nE\nS\nA. 80%\nB. 60%\nC. 40%\nD. 20%\nT\nIONS\n6 kWh is unit for\nB. Power.\nC. Efficiency D. None\nA. Energy\n1 hp =\nA. 476 W\nB. 550 W\nC. 746 W\nD. 1 ft.lb/s\n8 Hira weighing 500 N takes 90 s to reach the top of a hill 18 m high. Her\naverage muscle power is\nA. 2500 W. B. 100 W\nC. 32.8 W D. 3.24 W\n9 A machine is able to lift 200 N of concrete slab vertically up to a height of 30 m above the ground in 50 s. The average power of the machine is\nA. 1.33 W\nB. 60 W\nC. 120 W\nD. 6000 W\nPage 168\nUnit-6\nWork and Energy\nCONCEPTUAL QUESTIONS\nGive a brief response to the following questions.\nCan a centripetal force ever do work on an object? Explain.\nWhat happens to the kinetic energy of a bullet when it penetrates into a sand bag?\nA meteor enters into earth's atmosphere and burns. What happens to its kinetic energy?\nTwo bullets are fired at the same time with the same kinetic energy. If one bullet has twice the mass of the other, which has the greater speed and by what factor? Which can do the most work?\nCan an object have different amounts of gravitational potential energy if it remains at the same elevation?\nWhy do roads leading to the top of a mountain wind back and forth?\nWhich would have a greater effect on the kinetic energy of an object, doubling the mass or doubling the velocity?\nIf the speed of a particle triples, by what factor does its kinetic energy increase?\nThe motor of a crane uses power P to lift a steel beam. By what factor must the motor's power increase to lift the beam twice as high in half the time?\nCOMPREHENSIVE QUESTIONS\nGive an extended response to the following questions.\nDefine work and explain how work is calculated if force is applied at an\nangle.\nO Define kinetic energy. Derive the expression used for kinetic energy. What is potential energy? Prove that the gravitational potential energy of a body of mass mat a height h above the surface of earth is given by mgh. O State the law of conservation of energy and mass energy conversion\nrelation.\nExplain briefly major sources of energy. Such as fossil fuel, wind, solar,\nbiomass, nuclear and thermal energy.\nDefine and explain efficiency.\nO Define and explain power.\nPage 169\nUnit 6\nWork and Energy\nNUMERICAL QUESTIONS\nDetermine the work done in each of the following cases:\na) Kicking a soccer ball forward with a force of 40 N over a distance of 15 cm\nb) Lifting a 50 kg barbell straight up 1.95 m.\nO Calculate the velocity of a 1.2 kg falling star (meteorite) with 5.5 x 10' Jof\nenergy.\nCalculate the gravitational potential energy of a 2000 kg piano\na) resting on the floor b) with respect to the basement floor, 1.9 m below. An elevator weighing 5000 N is raised to a height of 15.0 m in 10.0s, how much power is developed?\nWhat power is required for a ski-hill chair lift that transports 500 people (average mass 65 kg) per hour to an increased elevation of 1200 m? How long will it take a 2750 W motor to lift a 385 kg sofa set to a sixth-story\nwindow 16.0m above?\nHow much work can a 2.0 hp motor do in 1.0 h?\nWEB LINKS\nwww.mowp.gov.pk/ http://www.aedb.org/\nPage 170\nWhat is common in\nice cube and water?\nCH\nUnit\n7\nPROPERTIES OF MATTER\nAfter studying this unit you should be able to:\n✓state kinetic molecular model of matter (solid, liquid and gas forms). H✓ describe briefly the fourth state of matter i.e., \"Plasma\".\nE define the term 'Density'.\nC\n✓compare the densities of a few solids, liquids and gases.\nK\n✓ define the term pressure (as a force acting normally on unit area).\n✓ explain how pressure varies with force and area in the context of everyday\nexamples.\nL\nexplain that the atmosphere exerts a pressure.\n✓ describe how the height of a liquid column may be used to measure the\nS atmospheric pressure.\nT\n✓ describe that atmospheric pressure decreases with the increase in height above\nthe Earth's surface.\n✓ explain that changes in atmospheric pressure in a region may indicate a change\nin the weather.\n✓state Pascal's law. Apply and demonstrate the use with examples of Pascal's law ✓state relation for pressure beneath a liquid surface to depth and to density i.e., (p=pgh) and solve problems using this equation.\nstate Archimedes principle. Determine the density of an object using Archimedes principle.\n✓ state the upthrust exerted by a liquid on a body.\n✓ state principle of floatation.\n✓ explain that a force may produce a change in size and shape of a body.\n✔define the terms Stress, Strain and Young's modulus.\n✔state Hooke's law and explain elastic limit.\nPage",
