          {
            "question": "Derive the mathematical formula for the Kinetic Energy of a moving body: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$.",
            "answer": "1. Physical Consideration:\nConsider a body of mass $m$ initially moving with velocity $v$.\nSuppose an opposing resistive force $F$ is applied to stop it.\nThe body experiences uniform retardation $a$ and comes to rest ($v_f = 0$) after covering distance $S$.\n\n2. Work-Kinetic Energy Formulation:\nThe kinetic energy of the body equals the total work it can perform against the opposing force before coming to rest:\n$E_k = W = F \\times S$  --- (Equation 1)\n\n3. Applying Newton's Second Law:\n$F = ma$  --- (Equation 2)\n\n4. Applying Third Equation of Motion:\n$2aS = v_f^2 - v_i^2$.\nHere $v_i = v$, $v_f = 0$, and acceleration is negative ($-a$):\n$2(-a)S = 0 - v^2$\n$-2aS = -v^2 \\implies 2aS = v^2 \\implies S = \\frac{v^2}{2a}$  --- (Equation 3)\n\n5. Substituting into Work Formula:\nSubstitute Eq. 2 and Eq. 3 into Eq. 1:\n$E_k = (ma) \\times \\left(\\frac{v^2}{2a}\\right)$.\nCanceling acceleration $a$:\n$E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$.\n\nConclusion: The kinetic energy of an object of mass $m$ moving at velocity $v$ is directly proportional to its mass and the square of its speed: $E_k = \\frac{1}{2}mv^2$.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 6 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "State the Law of Conservation of Energy. Prove that the total mechanical energy of a body falling freely under gravity is conserved at all points of its path.",
            "answer": "1. Statement: 'Energy cannot be created or destroyed, but can only be transformed from one form into another, such that total mechanical energy remains constant.'\n\n2. Mathematical Proof for a Freely Falling Body:\nConsider a body of mass $m$ resting at point A at height $h$ above the ground.\n\n- Case 1: At Point A (Top position at height $h$):\n  Velocity $v_A = 0$.\n  Kinetic Energy: $E_{kA} = \\frac{1}{2}m(0)^2 = 0$.\n  Potential Energy: $E_{pA} = mgh$.\n  Total Energy at A: $E_A = E_{kA} + E_{pA} = 0 + mgh = mgh$  --- (Eq. 1)\n\n- Case 2: At Point B (After falling distance $x$):\n  Height above ground: $h - x$.\n  From 3rd equation: $v_B^2 = v_A^2 + 2gx = 0 + 2gx = 2gx$.\n  Kinetic Energy: $E_{kB} = \\frac{1}{2}m v_B^2 = \\frac{1}{2}m(2gx) = mgx$.\n  Potential Energy: $E_{pB} = mg(h - x) = mgh - mgx$.\n  Total Energy at B: $E_B = E_{kB} + E_{pB} = mgx + (mgh - mgx) = mgh$  --- (Eq. 2)\n\n- Case 3: At Point C (Just before hitting the ground):\n  Height $h_C = 0 \\implies E_{pC} = 0$.\n  Total distance fallen = $h$.\n  From 3rd equation: $v_C^2 = 0 + 2gh = 2gh$.\n  Kinetic Energy: $E_{kC} = \\frac{1}{2}m(2gh) = mgh$.\n  Total Energy at C: $E_C = E_{kC} + E_{pC} = mgh + 0 = mgh$  --- (Eq. 3)\n\n3. Conclusion:\nComparing Equations 1, 2, and 3: $E_A = E_B = E_C = mgh$.\nAs the body falls, potential energy decreases while kinetic energy increases by the exact same amount. Total mechanical energy remains strictly conserved.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 6 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Discuss in detail the major conventional (fossil fuels, nuclear) and non-conventional renewable (solar, wind, hydro, biomass) energy resources of the world.",
            "answer": "1. Conventional / Non-Renewable Sources:\n- Fossil Fuels (Coal, Petroleum Oil, Natural Gas): Formed over millions of years from buried prehistoric organic matter. Burning them releases carbon dioxide ($CO_2$), nitrogen oxides, and sulfur dioxide, driving the greenhouse effect, acid rain, and global climate change. Reserves are finite.\n- Nuclear Energy: Harnesses heavy radioactive elements (Uranium-235) in nuclear fission reactors. A massive quantity of heat is released without greenhouse gas emissions, though safe storage of spent radioactive nuclear waste remains a technical challenge.\n\n2. Renewable / Non-Conventional Sources:\n- Solar Energy: Clean solar radiation harnessed via photovoltaic (PV) semiconductor panels to generate electricity directly, or via solar thermal collectors for water heating.\n- Hydroelectric Power: Potential energy of dammed water runs high-speed hydraulic turbines connected to electrical generators (e.g., Tarbela and Mangla dams in Pakistan), providing clean, low-cost electricity.\n- Wind Energy: Wind turbines extract kinetic energy from moving atmospheric air masses. Wind farms in coastal belts (like Jhimpir, Sindh) supply power to national electric grids.\n- Biomass & Biogas: Organic waste, sugarcane bagasse, and livestock manure decomposed in anaerobic digesters yield methane biogas for cooking and electricity generation.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 6 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Define and explain Efficiency. Why can no machine be 100% efficient? Discuss energy losses in typical machines using Sankey diagrams.",
            "answer": "1. Definition of Efficiency:\nEfficiency is the ratio of useful work or energy output delivered by a machine to the total energy input supplied to it, expressed as a percentage:\n$\\text{Efficiency} = \\frac{\\text{Useful Work Output}}{\\text{Total Energy Input}} \\times 100\\%$.\n\n2. Why 100% Efficiency is Impossible:\nAn 'ideal machine' with 100% efficiency would require zero energy losses. In the physical universe, friction between moving parts, aerodynamic air resistance, electrical wire resistance (Joule heating), and mechanical vibration inevitably dissipate a fraction of the input energy as wasted heat and sound. Therefore, useful output is always strictly less than input energy, making efficiency $< 100\\%$.\n\n3. Energy Flow in Machines (Sankey Flow):\n- Electric Lamp: 100 J input $\\implies$ 10 J useful light output + 90 J wasted heat (10% efficient).\n- Car Engine: 100 J chemical fuel input $\\implies$ 25 J mechanical motion + 75 J lost as radiator heat and exhaust gases (25% efficient).\n- Electric Motor: 100 J electrical input $\\implies$ 85 J rotational kinetic energy + 15 J lost as motor heat and humming sound (85% efficient).",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 6 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          },
          {
            "question": "Define Power. Derive its formula in terms of force and velocity. Explain all units of power (Watt, kilowatt, megawatt, horsepower) and the commercial unit of energy (kWh).",
            "answer": "1. Definition of Power:\nPower is defined as the time rate of doing work, or the rate at which energy is transferred: $P = \\frac{W}{t}$.\n\n2. Power in Terms of Force and Velocity:\nSince work is $W = F \\times S$:\n$P = \\frac{F \\times S}{t} = F \\times \\left(\\frac{S}{t}\\right)$.\nSince average velocity $v = \\frac{S}{t}$:\n$P = F \\times v$.\n\n3. Units of Power:\n- Watt (W): The SI unit of power. One Watt is the power when 1 Joule of work is performed per second ($1\\text{ W} = 1\\text{ J/s}$).\n- Multiples: Kilowatt ($1\\text{ kW} = 1000\\text{ W} = 10^3\\text{ W}$), Megawatt ($1\\text{ MW} = 10^6\\text{ W}$), Gigawatt ($1\\text{ GW} = 10^9\\text{ W}$).\n- Horsepower (hp): Traditional British engineering unit: $1\\text{ hp} = 746\\text{ W}$.\n\n4. Commercial Unit of Energy (Kilowatt-hour, kWh):\nThe Joule is too small for practical utility billing. Electrical energy consumed is measured in kilowatt-hours (kWh):\n$1\\text{ kWh} = 1\\text{ kW} \\times 1\\text{ hour} = 1000\\text{ W} \\times 3600\\text{ s} = 3.6 \\times 10^6\\text{ J} = 3.6\\text{ MJ}$.",
            "marks": 7,
            "slo": "Unit 6 Comprehensive Learning Outcome"
          }
