          "text": "7.10 STRESS, STRAIN AND YOUNG'S MODULUS\n\nA wire under tension will extend. Its extension depends on a number of\n\nfactors such as the tension, its diameter and length and the material of the wire\n\nSuppose that a wire of length land cross-sectional area 'A' is stretched by: force Fand the extension produced is x as shown in figure 7.10.\n\nStress:\n\nThe stress applied to the wire is defined as the force applied per unit area of cross-section to produce deformation. Mathematically\n\nFigure 7.10\n\nForce\n\nStress =\n\nArea of crossection\n\nor\n\nstress\n\nF\n\nA\n\n7.7\n\nStrain:\n\nThe unit for stress is N m2 or pascal.\n\nAs a result of the wire being stretched, it is under strain. The strain is defined as the extension per unit length. Mathematically\n\nor\n\nExtension Original Length\n\nStrain =\n\nX\n\nstrain =\n\n7.8\n\nSince strain is the ratio of two lengths, it does not have a unit.\n\nLAB WORK\n\nTo find the density of a liquid using 5 ml syringe (instead of dimity bottle).\n\nYoung's Modulus:\n\nThe strain produced in a stretched wire is proportional to the stress within\n\nPage\n\n190\n\nProperties of Matter\n\nthe limit of proportionality. Within this limit, the ratio is a constant whose value depends on the material of the wire, and is known as the Young's modulus of the\n\nmaterial. Mathematically\n\nor\n\nStress & Strain\n\nStress Constant > Strain\n\nConstant of proportionality is called Young's modulus is denoted by Y\n\nStress = Young's Modulus x Strain\n\nrearranging the above equation, we get\n\nModulus for Selected Materials\n\nYoung Modulus,\n\nMaterial\n\nY, (Pa)\n\nAluminum\n\n7.0 × 1010\n\nBrass\n\n9.0 × 1010\n\nCopper\n\n11 x 1010\n\nStress\n\nYoung's Modulus =\n\nCrown glass\n\n6.0 × 1010\n\nStrain\n\nIron\n\n21 × 1010\n\nYoung's modulus is denoted by Yand its unit is Nm2, putting values of stress and strain, we\n\nLead\n\n1.6 × 1010\n\nget\n\nY =\n\nF/A\n\nNickel\n\n21 × 1010\n\nX\n\nSteel\n\n20 x 1010\n\nFxl\n\nrearranging\n\nY =\n\n7.9\n\nAxx\n\nYoung Modulus for different materials are given in the table 7.2.\n\nStress strain curves:\n\nStress and strain curves are measured by stress tester.\n\nThe applied stress is increased and the change in length is noted. The values are then printed on graph. A typical graph for metal is shown in the figure 7.11.\n\nHere, Point A, is the limit of proportionality, the limit up to which Hooke's law is obeyed called proportional limit.\n\nLAB WORK\n\nFigure 7.11 Stress strain curve\n\nStress\n\nol\n\nElastic region B\n\nC\n\nA\n\nPlastic\n\nregion\n\nStrain\n\nTo study the relation between load and extension (helical spring) by drawing\n\ngraph.\n\nPage\n\n191\n\nProperties of Matter\n\npoint C is the maximum stress wire can with stand without breaking and point D, is Point B, is the elastic limit, the limit up to which material shows elastic behavior,\n\nthe breaking point, where material breaks.\n\nExample 7.5\n\nYOUNG'S MODULUS\n\nAwire of length 5.0 m and area of 0.03 m2 is stretched 0.15 m within elastic limit, by hanging weight of 270 N. Calculate stress, strain and Young's modulus of\n\nmaterial of wire.\n\nS GIVEN:\n\nO length = 5.0m\n\nL area A=0.03 m2\n\nU extension x = 0.15m\n\nTweight force F = 270 N\n\nF\n\nREQUIRED:\n\nstress=?\n\nstrain = ?\n\nYoung's modulus Y = ?\n\na) By definition of stress\n\nstress =\n\n=-\n\nO\n\nA\n\nN\n\nputting values\n\nstress:\n\n270N 0.03m2\n\nstress=9x103 Nm-2\n\nAnswer\n\nb) By definition of strain\n\nstrain =\n\nX\n\nputting values\n\n0.15m\n\nstrain =\n\n5.0m\n\nstrain = 0.03\n\nAnswer\n\nc) Young's Modulus is\n\nYoung's Modulus\n\n·Stress\n\nStrain\n\nputting values\n\nY = 9.0×103Nm-2\n\n0:03\n\ntherefore\n\nY = 3.0×105Nm-2\n\nAnswer\n\nAssignment 7.5 WIRE STRETCHED\n\nAn elastic wire of length 2 m and cross sectional area 0.02 m2 is stretched 0.10m\n\nby a 300 N weight. Calculate the Young modulus of the material.\n\nK Density: Mass per unit volume\n\nE Pressure: Force applied per unit area\n\nBarometer: Device used to measure atmospheric pressure\n\nPascal's Principle: if the pressure at one point of a confined fluid is increased by P an amount, the pressure increases by the same amount at all other parts 0 throughout the fluid.\n\nArchimedes' principle: A body when completely or partially immersed in a fluid will experience an upthrust equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the Tbody. This upthrust will bring about an apparent loss of weight of the body.\n\nP\n\nElasticity: The property of a body which enables the body to regain its original dimension when the deforming force acting on the body is removed.\n\nHooke's law: Within elastic limits the extension (or compression) is directly proportional to the force applied.\n\nStress: The force applied per unit area of cross-section to produce deformation. Strain: the extension per unit length.\n\nYoung's Modulus: The ratio of stress to strain.\n\nGROUP-A\n\nR TYRE PRESSURE: Research air pressures in different tyre types (bicycles, motor Obikes, cars and trucks). Why are the pressures different for different automobile\n\ntyres? Prepare a presentation to be presented in physics class.\n\nECTS\n\nGROUP - B\n\nSUBMARINES: Prepare a chart on submarine technology, what is the science of their\n\nsinking and raising up.\n\nGROUP-C\n\nWEATHER IN PAKISTAN: What is the organization that monitors and predicts weather in Pakistan, write a research article for the school library.\n\nGROUP-D\n\nPRESSURIZED CABINS: Write an article for school magazine on why submarine and aircraft cabins are pressurized at certain level. What would happen if the cabins are\n\nnot maintained at constant pressure.\n\nGROUP-E\n\nDENSITIES: Find a spring balance and use Archimedes Principle to determine the densities of few objects. Compare the calculated density results with the table and\n\nPage\n\n193"
