            "question": "Define specific heat capacity. Derive the formula $\\Delta Q = m c \\Delta T$. Explain the great importance of the high specific heat capacity of water in daily life and climate moderation.",
            "answer": "1. Definition: Specific heat capacity ($c$) is defined as the amount of thermal energy required to raise the temperature of $1\\text{ kg}$ of a substance by $1\\text{ K}$ (or $1^\\circ\\text{C}$).\n\n2. Mathematical Derivation:\nThe quantity of heat $\\Delta Q$ required to warm a body depends on:\n- Mass of the body ($m$): $\\Delta Q \\propto m$  --- (Eq. 1)\n- Rise in temperature ($\\Delta T$): $\\Delta Q \\propto \\Delta T$  --- (Eq. 2)\nCombining both proportionalities:\n$\\Delta Q \\propto m \\Delta T \\implies \\Delta Q = m c \\Delta T$.\nWhere $c = \\frac{\\Delta Q}{m \\Delta T}$ is the specific heat capacity. SI unit: $\\text{J}/(\\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{K})$.\n\n3. Importance of the High Specific Heat of Water ($4200\\text{ J/kg}\\cdot\\text{K}$):\nWater has one of the highest specific heat capacities of any substance on Earth:\n- Engine Coolant: Water can absorb tremendous quantities of heat from combustion cylinders with only a moderate rise in temperature. Thus, it is universally used in automobile radiators and industrial cooling towers.\n- Central Heating Systems: Hot water circulating through domestic radiator pipes carries large amounts of heat to warm rooms efficiently.\n- Climate Moderation & Sea Breezes: The ocean heats and cools far more slowly than adjacent landmasses. During the day, land heats rapidly, drawing cool sea breezes inland. At night, land cools quickly while the sea stays warm, creating land breezes. This moderates coastal weather, preventing extreme temperature swings.",
