          "text": "9.4 RADIATION OF HEAT\n\nWhen we sit next to an open campfire, Figure 9.7 a Soldering the heat from the fire does not reach us by conduction because air is a bad conductor of heat. It does not reach us by convection because hot air rises up. In this case, the heat reaches us by radiation of heat. The hot fire radiates heat rays just as light rays. When these rays fall on anything they make that object hotter figure 9.7 a.\n\nSun heat reaches us after passing through millions of kilometers in empty space. Sun heat also warms up anything on which it falls. Neither conduction nor convection can take place in vacuum as no material is present\n\nFigure 9.7 b Soldering\n\nfor the transfer of heat through conduction or convection. Sun heat reaches us by radiation of heat figure 9.7 b. Based on the above observations, we define radiation of heat as The heat transfer from a hotter place to a colder place with or without having a material medium in between is called radiation of heat.\n\nUnlike conduction and convection transfer of heat by radiation, does not necessarily require a material medium.\n\n9.4.1 MECHANISM OF RADIATION OF HEAT\n\nThe mechanism of radiation is energy transfer by electromagnetic waves. Electromagnetic radiation comes from accelerating electric charges. On a molecular level, that's what happens as objects warm up their molecules vibrate harder and harder, causing acceleration of electric charges which emits those radiation. Heat energy transferred through radiation is as familiar as the light; in fact, it is the light but not visible or barely visible. Electromagnetic waves (radiation) can transfer energy via vacuum or empty space as well as via a\n\n243\n\nPage\n\nTransfer of Heat\n\nmaterial medium like glass.\n\nEvery object around us is continually radiating, unless its temperature is at absolute zero 0 K (which is a little unlikely because you can't physically get to a temperature of absolute zero, with no molecular movement). For example a scoop of ice cream has temperature of about 237 K, therefore it radiates.\n\nFigure 9.7 c Infrared radiation\n\n923\n\n81.4\n\nEven we radiate all the time, but that radiation isn't visible as light because it's in the infrared part of the spectrum. However, that light is visible to infrared scopes, as you've probably seen in the movies or on television.\n\nFigure 9.7 d ELECTROMAGNETIC SPECTRUM\n\n0.0001 nm 0.01 nm 1 nm 100 nm 1000 nm 1 mm\n\n1 m\n\n1 km\n\nV+BUYOR\n\nGamma rays\n\nX\n\nUltra\n\nrays\n\nViolet light Infrared\n\nVisible\n\nRadio Radio waves\n\nwaves\n\n100 x 106 m\n\n1 x 103m\n\nINFRARED\n\nwwwww\n\n9.4.2 GOOD AND BAD EMITTERS AND ABSORBERS\n\nSome surfaces are better absorbers Activity 9.4 RADIATION OF HEAT\n\nof radiation than others, a black colour absorbs most and white reflects most. A dull\n\nblack kettle absorbs heat better than a\n\nsilver kettle. Standing in the sun, a black car warms up more quickly than car of any other colour.\n\nhot copper sheet with one side polished and other blackened\n\nback of\n\nhands towards the sheet\n\nSome surfaces are better at If you hold the backs of your hands on either emitting radiation than others when heated side of a hot copper sheet that has one side or allowed to cool. A black saucepan cools polished and the other side blackened as down quickly than any other. Hot water in a black surface is a better emitter of radiation shown in figure, it will be found that the dull\n\nkettle covered with soot cools faster than a\n\nthan the shiny one.\n\nTransfer of Hear\n\nsimilar kettle having shining surface.\n\nIn general a good absorber is a good radiator and a poor absorber is a\n\npoor radiator.\n\nThe rate of energy transfer by radiation is affected by:\n\nColour and texture of the surface\n\n0\n\nSurface temperature\n\n0\n\nSurface area\n\n0\n\nColor & texture of surface: In general dark coloured (e.g. black) surfaces emit or absorb radiation energy more effectively. The color of surface affects its reflectivity and, therefore shiny coloured (e.g. white or silvery) and metallic surfaces emit or absorb radiation energy slowly since they reflect the radiation. The cooling fins on the heat exchanger at the back of a refrigerator are painted black so that they lose heat more quickly.\n\nThe surface texture could vary from smooth to rough. A rough textured surface (for example a grit finish with large aggregate size) causes self shading. It also increases the area for radiation, which will make the heat transfer faster. Teapots are made with shiny, polish metal in order to reduce heat lost by radiation. Thus, a shiny metal teapot keep liquid warmer for longer time.\n\nSurface temperature. The higher the temperature of the surface of the object relative to the surrounding temperature, the higher the rate of radiation. For example a red hot iron piece radiate more heat energy because of its high temperature.\n\nFigure 9.8\n\nb\n\na\n\nSurface Area, The greater the surface area of the object, the faster is the rate of heat transfer from it. If two objects have the same mass and materials, the object with the larger surface area will have a higher rate of radiation.\n\nThe big ears of the African elephant provide a large surface area for the giant mammal to cool off quickly in hot weather.\n\nTransfer of Heat\n\nD.\n\nThermos Flask:\n\nFigure 9.8\n\nTHERMOS FLASK\n\nOuter\n\nInner glass wall\n\nSilver film\n\nglass wall\n\nThe vessel which is used to prevent heat transfer due to conduction, convection and radiation is called thermos flask figure 9.8. It consists of a double- walled glass vessel silvered on the inside.\n\nHot or cold liquid\n\nStopper\n\nGasket\n\nVacuum\n\n(c)\n\nThe purpose of the slivering is to reflect all radiant heat trying to enter or leave the vessel. The space between the walls is highly evacuated (vacuum is created) to prevent convection. The glass, being a poor conductor minimizes conduction of heat as well. The heat loss through the flask is so small that a hot liquid placed in the flask will remain hot for a very long time. A cold thing placed in the flask will remain cold for a long time because flow of heat from the outside will also be very small.\n\nA thermos bottle minimizes energy transfer due to convection, conduction, and radiation."
