            "q": "Prove that the motion of a mass attached to a spring executing horizontal oscillations is Simple Harmonic Motion, and derive expressions for its time period and frequency.",
            "ans": "1. Proof of SHM:\n- Consider a block of mass m attached to an ideal massless horizontal spring of spring constant k resting on a frictionless table.\n- When pulled by displacement x from mean position O, an elastic restoring force acts: F = -kx (Hooke's Law).\n- By Newton's second law: F = ma.\n- Equating both: ma = -kx => a = -(k/m)x.\n- Since k and m are constants, (k/m) is constant. Hence: a ∝ -x.\n- This satisfies the defining condition of Simple Harmonic Motion: acceleration is directly proportional to displacement and directed towards the mean position.\n\n2. Time Period and Frequency:\n- In SHM, acceleration can be expressed in terms of angular frequency ω as a = -ω²x.\n- Comparing a = -(k/m)x with a = -ω²x gives: ω² = k/m => ω = √(k/m).\n- Since angular frequency ω = 2π/T = 2πf:\n  * Time Period: T = 2π / ω = 2π√(m/k)\n  * Frequency: f = 1 / T = (1/2π)√(k/m)."
