            "q": "Describe the construction and working of a Cathode-Ray Oscilloscope (CRO) with a neat labelled diagram. What are its principal uses?",
            "ans": "1. Principle: Uses an accelerated, focused electron beam deflected by electric fields between X and Y plates to trace voltage waveforms on a fluorescent screen.\n2. Main Parts:\n- Electron Gun: Filament heats cathode to emit electrons by thermionic emission. Negative control grid regulates beam brightness. Anodes at high positive potential accelerate and focus electrons into a sharp beam.\n- Deflection System: Y-plates deflect beam vertically according to the input signal under test. X-plates deflect beam horizontally using a linear sawtooth time-base voltage, providing the time axis.\n- Fluorescent Screen: Coated with phosphor that glows when hit by electrons, producing a bright visible trace.\n3. Uses: Displaying AC/DC waveforms, measuring peak-to-peak voltages, determining unknown frequencies, measuring short time intervals, and medical monitoring (ECG/EEG)."
